WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.820
You know, when we usually think about a perfect, unbroken historical record, there is this kind of automatic, deeply ingrained expectation of inherent value.

00:10.260 --> 00:11.080
Right, yeah.

00:11.140 --> 00:15.060
We naturally assume that if something is flawless, it just has to be useful.

00:15.340 --> 00:15.720
Exactly.

00:16.900 --> 00:21.980
Like, imagine, if you will, that someone builds a perfectly secure, completely indestructible vault.

00:21.980 --> 00:29.040
We are talking, you know, impenetrable titanium walls, biometric locks, lasers, just a completely closed system.

00:29.220 --> 00:29.320
Yeah.

00:30.020 --> 00:39.560
And simply because nothing can ever break into this vault and nothing inside it can ever be secretly altered or, you know, stolen, we assume it's doing something incredibly important for society.

00:40.260 --> 00:43.100
Well, the security itself feels like an accomplishment, right?

00:43.140 --> 00:44.800
It's this binary state in our minds.

00:44.880 --> 00:46.700
It is either tamper-proof or it isn't.

00:46.780 --> 00:48.540
And if it is, we applaud the engineering.

00:48.720 --> 00:48.920
Right.

00:49.540 --> 00:50.360
And it is comforting.

00:50.360 --> 00:53.220
I mean, we fundamentally like things to be secure.

00:53.480 --> 00:55.140
We like history to be immutable.

00:55.140 --> 01:02.000
But then you step into the world of distributed ledgers, blockchains, and digital tokens.

01:02.600 --> 01:04.440
And that vault metaphor gets a little weird.

01:04.880 --> 01:05.860
It gets really weird.

01:05.860 --> 01:18.680
Because what if you have this perfect, mathematically flawless vault and you spend billions of dollars maintaining the lasers and the titanium walls, but when you actually look inside, it is just full of empty cardboard boxes.

01:18.680 --> 01:23.820
Yeah, that is, I mean, that is the absolute definition of a technological illusion.

01:24.380 --> 01:28.940
A machine functioning perfectly to achieve a goal that basically has no external meaning.

01:29.140 --> 01:29.240
Right.

01:29.240 --> 01:39.580
And that structural tension between a machine doing exactly what it was programmed to do and a machine actually contributing something to the human experience is what we are unpacking today.

01:40.100 --> 01:42.220
Which is why I am so excited.

01:42.920 --> 01:44.180
Welcome to today's Deep Dive.

01:44.340 --> 01:56.100
If you are joining us, and yes, I am talking directly to you, the curious learner who is sitting right here at the table with us as the third person in this conversation, we have a genuinely mind-bending mission today.

01:56.500 --> 01:57.160
We really do.

01:57.160 --> 02:02.440
We are looking at a paper published in October, 2026 by Flyxion Independent Researcher.

02:02.860 --> 02:08.500
It is titled, Consistency Without Contribution, Fees, Selection, and the Accounting of Work.

02:08.940 --> 02:12.160
Yeah, and the text asks a devastatingly simple question.

02:12.680 --> 02:19.360
Just because a machine perfectly records its own history, does it mean it is actually doing anything useful for the real world?

02:20.020 --> 02:20.800
It's a great question.

02:20.800 --> 02:31.520
I have been looking forward to this because this whole space, you know, cryptocurrencies, digital ledgers, decentralized finance, it usually feels completely overwhelmed by jargon.

02:31.940 --> 02:32.380
Oh, absolutely.

02:32.940 --> 02:42.120
People throw around terms like immutable and decentralized and trustless as if those words automatically magically equal economic productivity.

02:42.120 --> 02:42.560
Right.

02:42.660 --> 02:44.200
They use them as synonyms for value.

02:44.620 --> 02:45.000
Exactly.

02:45.700 --> 02:53.660
But our mission today is to separate the brilliant cryptography of these ledgers from the blunt reality of their actual economic usefulness.

02:54.120 --> 03:04.220
We are going to impartially explore the mathematical proofs, the cash flow dynamics, the environmental costs, and the structural survivor problems outlined in this text.

03:04.220 --> 03:07.340
We are just keeping our focus entirely on what the evidence shows.

03:07.440 --> 03:10.540
No hype, no vitriol, just the actual mechanics of value.

03:10.780 --> 03:10.980
Yes.

03:11.520 --> 03:15.520
So, to understand the mechanics of value, where does Flyxion start?

03:16.000 --> 03:20.100
Well, Flyxion begins by demanding we look at the foundation of the technology itself.

03:20.280 --> 03:26.080
We have to start with the ledger to understand what it actually achieves before we evaluate its economic impact.

03:26.320 --> 03:27.120
Okay, that makes sense.

03:27.120 --> 03:33.900
So, in 2008, Satoshi Nakamoto proposed the append-only chain of blocks, which we now just call the blockchain.

03:34.420 --> 03:42.140
It is vital to acknowledge right up front that this solved a massive real-world problem in computer science, which is the double-spend problem.

03:42.660 --> 03:47.680
Let's break that down for a second, because understanding the double-spend problem is, like, crucial.

03:47.900 --> 03:48.360
Yeah, go ahead.

03:48.760 --> 03:53.120
In the physical world, if I hand you a $20 bill, I don't have it anymore.

03:53.780 --> 03:54.960
I can't spend it again.

03:54.960 --> 03:58.260
But in the digital world, money is just data.

03:58.780 --> 04:01.180
It's a file, and files can be copied.

04:01.360 --> 04:01.460
Right.

04:01.940 --> 04:13.220
So, traditionally, we needed a bank, a trusted intermediary, to basically look at my account, verify I have the $20 deducted for my balance, and add it to yours.

04:13.400 --> 04:13.860
Exactly.

04:13.860 --> 04:24.760
And Satoshi Nakamoto's invention allowed parties with absolutely no prior relationship and no trusted intermediary to maintain a shared history of transactions.

04:25.460 --> 04:26.820
Which was revolutionary.

04:27.160 --> 04:28.380
It really was.

04:28.680 --> 04:36.860
By linking blocks of data cryptographically and requiring the network to reach a consensus, the system secures what the paper calls consistency.

04:37.360 --> 04:38.460
Consistency, right.

04:38.460 --> 04:39.560
Yeah, consistency.

04:40.200 --> 04:44.740
Every present claim to a digital token perfectly agrees with every recorded past transfer.

04:45.400 --> 04:49.000
No one can secretly rewrite the ledger to say they have money they already spent.

04:49.120 --> 04:51.940
Which, I mean, is an incredible feat of engineering.

04:52.400 --> 04:54.820
But I want to push back a little on the premise here.

04:54.900 --> 04:55.080
Sure.

04:55.080 --> 04:59.800
Isn't a tamper-proof log inherently valuable just by existing?

05:00.420 --> 05:02.080
Let's go back to my vault analogy.

05:03.020 --> 05:14.860
Even if the vault is holding cardboard boxes right now, the fact that I have an indestructible vault that perfectly, securely logs every single time a box is moved from one shelf to another,

05:15.660 --> 05:20.060
well, isn't that a technological leap that carries its own intrinsic value?

05:20.060 --> 05:24.940
Well, the invention is mathematically real, and the paper validates that.

05:25.540 --> 05:34.040
But if we connect this to the bigger picture, we have to distinguish between a system being admissible and a system being valuable.

05:34.800 --> 05:36.600
Admissible versus valuable.

05:36.760 --> 05:37.060
Right.

05:37.120 --> 05:37.340
Okay.

05:37.700 --> 05:42.840
Admissibility simply means the ledger accepts a transaction according to its programmed rules.

05:43.180 --> 05:44.360
It certifies movement.

05:44.360 --> 05:49.480
It confirms that, say, user A sent a token to user B, and the math checks out.

05:49.480 --> 05:49.940
Right.

05:50.020 --> 05:50.760
The system worked.

05:50.940 --> 05:51.280
It worked.

05:51.640 --> 05:57.400
But it does not certify that the movement was worth the resources it consumed to perform it.

05:57.660 --> 05:57.840
Ah.

05:58.400 --> 06:03.740
So, to use the analogy, the vault's log perfectly records that a cardboard box was moved.

06:04.340 --> 06:09.340
But if moving that box required burning a pile of coal to power the vault's security system,

06:09.340 --> 06:14.040
and the box itself contains nothing that anyone in the outside world needs,

06:14.720 --> 06:16.340
well, the perfect log hasn't created value.

06:17.140 --> 06:17.900
No, it hasn't.

06:17.900 --> 06:20.960
It has just flawlessly documented a waste of resources.

06:21.620 --> 06:23.500
That is the core distinction right there.

06:23.800 --> 06:26.100
Consistency protects the recorded history.

06:26.880 --> 06:32.400
It says absolutely nothing about whether the events in the log actually did anything for anyone outside the log.

06:32.400 --> 06:36.460
A flawless ledger might just be recording the useless shuffling of internal claims.

06:37.180 --> 06:38.900
That is such a profound way to look at it.

06:38.900 --> 06:46.060
So, if perfectly recording the movement of data isn't inherently useful, we are left with a massive problem,

06:46.140 --> 06:49.100
which is, how do we actually figure out what is useful?

06:49.700 --> 06:51.260
Yeah, that's the big question.

06:51.720 --> 06:54.780
Because usefulness feels like a really subjective term, right?

06:54.780 --> 06:57.820
What I find useful, someone else might find totally pointless.

06:58.240 --> 07:04.160
And the subjectivity of value is exactly why Flyxion constructs a rigorous mathematical definition for it.

07:04.540 --> 07:10.620
The paper defines productivity as net work reduction, which is represented by the Greek symbol delta sub T.

07:10.840 --> 07:11.820
Delta sub T. Okay.

07:11.820 --> 07:16.620
To determine if a system is actually productive, you cannot just look at the system in isolation.

07:17.300 --> 07:20.240
You have to identify a specific bundle of needs.

07:20.840 --> 07:21.740
Let's ground that.

07:22.380 --> 07:26.700
Like, a bundle of needs could be the ability for a migrant worker to send money back to their family.

07:26.820 --> 07:27.080
Yes.

07:27.420 --> 07:30.980
It could be the ability for a business to store its operating capital safely.

07:31.400 --> 07:35.180
Or, you know, the ability to allocate loans to new businesses.

07:35.640 --> 07:39.580
Crucially, this bundle of needs must exist independently of the system itself.

07:39.580 --> 07:47.620
If a system invents a completely artificial problem, like a video game requiring you to mine a digital gem to open a digital door,

07:48.120 --> 07:50.880
solving that problem doesn't count as real-world productivity.

07:51.180 --> 07:53.120
Because the need wouldn't exist without the game.

07:53.440 --> 07:54.000
Exactly.

07:54.520 --> 07:57.220
The need has to exist whether the technology exists or not.

07:57.660 --> 08:01.340
So, how does the net work reduction formula actually measure this?

08:01.900 --> 08:07.960
Productivity only happens if a system reduces the resource cost of delivering that specific bundle of needs,

08:07.960 --> 08:11.380
compared to the cheapest, accessible, real-world alternative.

08:11.920 --> 08:14.080
We call that alternative the counterfactual.

08:14.480 --> 08:15.600
The counterfactual, right.

08:15.940 --> 08:16.160
Right.

08:16.400 --> 08:19.540
You look at the cost of the best alternative that does not use the ledger.

08:19.840 --> 08:21.980
Then you subtract the cost of using the ledger.

08:22.360 --> 08:26.300
That difference is your net work reduction, your delta sub t.

08:26.580 --> 08:26.840
Okay.

08:27.020 --> 08:27.580
I think I follow.

08:27.960 --> 08:33.800
So, if the number is positive, meaning the ledger required less work and fewer resources to achieve the exact same goal,

08:34.300 --> 08:35.220
the system is productive.

08:35.220 --> 08:37.880
Let me make sure I'm translating this math correctly.

08:38.580 --> 08:44.800
If I want to send $100 overseas, and a traditional bank wire costs me $5 and takes two days,

08:45.100 --> 08:47.080
that is my counterfactual cost.

08:47.340 --> 08:47.700
Correct.

08:47.980 --> 08:53.140
If a digital ledger can send that same $100 with the same level of security,

08:53.320 --> 08:58.840
but it only costs $1 and takes 10 minutes, the net work reduction is positive.

08:59.360 --> 09:02.960
The system has genuinely saved the world $4 of effort.

09:02.960 --> 09:05.580
That is the mechanic of the formula, yes.

09:05.900 --> 09:08.520
But here is where the math gets incredibly strict.

09:08.900 --> 09:12.160
We have to be meticulous about whose costs we are counting.

09:12.660 --> 09:13.140
What do you mean?

09:13.640 --> 09:19.200
Well, the paper specifies we must calculate these costs across what it calls the global domain,

09:19.720 --> 09:22.140
represented mathematically as omega global.

09:22.460 --> 09:22.720
Okay.

09:22.800 --> 09:23.140
Hold on.

09:23.940 --> 09:25.040
Global domain.

09:25.040 --> 09:27.760
Who exactly is in omega global?

09:27.860 --> 09:33.600
Because usually when I buy a service, I only care about the price I pay and the profit the company makes.

09:34.140 --> 09:34.960
And that's the trap.

09:35.460 --> 09:40.580
Omega global includes everyone affected by the system, whether they are a user of the system or not.

09:40.580 --> 09:41.960
And this is vital.

09:41.960 --> 09:42.660
Oh, wow.

09:42.780 --> 09:42.920
Yeah.

09:43.220 --> 09:46.720
If a system looks incredibly cheap and efficient for the people trading on it,

09:46.800 --> 09:53.200
but it achieves that low cost by dumping massive external costs like severe environmental damage

09:53.200 --> 10:01.320
or draining the local power grid or creating mountains of toxic electronic waste onto people who are entirely outside the system,

10:01.480 --> 10:03.420
it is not actually productive.

10:03.780 --> 10:04.980
It's just shifting the accounting.

10:04.980 --> 10:06.720
It is pure redistribution.

10:07.460 --> 10:13.220
If a system just moves claims around and shifts the physical costs of doing so onto non-participants,

10:13.400 --> 10:16.160
the net work reduction is zero.

10:16.540 --> 10:22.900
Or, you know, in reality, it is deeply negative once you factor in the physical resources consumed to run the servers.

10:23.380 --> 10:26.940
So it's easy to look at a system, see waste, and assume it's bad.

10:27.320 --> 10:30.060
Or look at a system, see high activity, and assume it's good.

10:30.060 --> 10:35.740
But we have to measure what that waste is preventing or what that activity is displacing.

10:36.880 --> 10:41.300
It reminds me of the paper's analogy, which I'm calling the potato chip bag principle.

10:41.760 --> 10:45.960
I think that analogy perfectly clarifies the discipline Flyxion is demanding.

10:46.620 --> 10:46.860
Yeah.

10:46.960 --> 10:50.040
So if you're listening right now, picture a standard bag of potato chips.

10:50.640 --> 10:54.820
You buy it at the store, you open it up, and to a casual observer, it looks like a total scam.

10:55.200 --> 10:55.520
Right.

10:55.700 --> 10:57.820
Because the bag is mostly empty air.

10:58.120 --> 10:58.520
Exactly.

10:58.520 --> 11:03.520
You look at that empty volume, and you naturally infer that it is pure waste.

11:04.340 --> 11:06.740
It's an unproductive use of plastic in shipping space.

11:07.480 --> 11:11.960
But that gas inside the bag, which is usually nitrogen, serves a highly specific function.

11:12.420 --> 11:15.500
It prevents the fragile chips from being crushed into dust during transit,

11:15.680 --> 11:19.760
and it slows down the oxidation process so the food doesn't spoil on the shelf.

11:20.060 --> 11:20.280
Right.

11:20.380 --> 11:24.760
So if you force the manufacturer to use a smaller, vacuum-sealed bag with no gas,

11:25.420 --> 11:28.220
the breakage and spoilage rates would absolutely skyrocket.

11:28.220 --> 11:29.000
Yes, they would.

11:29.000 --> 11:33.200
You would actually end up increasing the total amount of work, farming, and resources needed

11:33.200 --> 11:36.820
to deliver a given quantity of edible intact food to a consumer.

11:36.820 --> 11:42.640
So what looks totally unproductive locally, an empty pocket of air, is actually contributing

11:42.640 --> 11:44.420
to the function of the larger arrangement.

11:44.420 --> 11:47.920
It reduces the network required to feed people.

11:48.720 --> 11:51.940
And we have to apply that exact same discipline to digital ledgers.

11:52.140 --> 11:52.700
We really do.

11:52.700 --> 11:59.540
We cannot call an idle or seemingly empty component of a ledger waste without first checking what

11:59.540 --> 12:01.060
it preserves, like the nitrogen.

12:01.440 --> 12:01.560
Right.

12:01.800 --> 12:07.680
But conversely, and this is where the paper's critique cuts incredibly deep, we cannot call

12:07.680 --> 12:13.220
a very busy, highly active network productive without checking what it displaces.

12:13.580 --> 12:14.480
Oh, that's the flip side.

12:14.880 --> 12:15.180
Yes.

12:15.180 --> 12:21.400
If a ledger is processing millions of transactions a second, we cannot just applaud the activity.

12:21.400 --> 12:26.200
We have to evaluate that activity against the accessible counterfactuals.

12:26.740 --> 12:32.320
We have to ask, what real-world alternative did this massive flurry of activity displace?

12:32.880 --> 12:36.460
What independent human need did it serve cheaper and better?

12:36.460 --> 12:42.600
And if the answer is none, if it didn't displace anything, and it just consumed electricity and

12:42.600 --> 12:47.180
hardware to shuffle tokens back and forth, then according to Proposition 1 in the text,

12:47.640 --> 12:49.660
it is just pure redistribution.

12:50.040 --> 12:51.480
It's an illusion of work.

12:52.020 --> 12:55.040
Which naturally forces us to ask a really uncomfortable question.

12:55.540 --> 13:01.160
If a massive, multi-billion dollar system isn't producing any real work, what is happening

13:01.160 --> 13:03.500
to all the actual money that people are pouring into it?

13:03.880 --> 13:06.360
That leads directly to Proposition 2.

13:06.460 --> 13:09.000
Which is the conservation of net cash position.

13:09.320 --> 13:10.660
Okay, walk me through this.

13:10.920 --> 13:12.180
Well, this is not a theory.

13:12.480 --> 13:16.980
It is an accounting identity, which means it is a mathematical certainty within its defined

13:16.980 --> 13:17.380
boundaries.

13:17.900 --> 13:22.180
If you take a closed group of participants who are trading digital tokens on a network,

13:22.760 --> 13:27.400
their aggregate net cash position, meaning the total amount of real-world money they can

13:27.400 --> 13:33.860
collectively withdraw from the system, is exactly equal to minus the costs they paid to outsiders.

13:34.420 --> 13:35.860
Wait, minus the costs?

13:35.860 --> 13:38.960
The total cash position of the group is always negative.

13:39.720 --> 13:43.060
Let's walk through this mechanically, because I want to make sure the listener can visualize

13:43.060 --> 13:43.480
this.

13:43.800 --> 13:43.900
Sure.

13:44.480 --> 13:48.060
Imagine a completely closed room, representing a token exchange.

13:48.580 --> 13:52.780
Ten people walk into the room, and they each bring $100 of real cash.

13:53.280 --> 13:55.380
There is a total of $1,000 in the room.

13:56.140 --> 13:58.900
Now, they start trading digital tokens among themselves.

13:58.900 --> 14:00.660
And the tokens are just data.

14:01.140 --> 14:02.120
They don't generate dividends.

14:02.380 --> 14:03.200
They don't produce goods.

14:03.540 --> 14:03.780
Correct.

14:04.720 --> 14:09.360
As they trade, they have to pay network transaction fees to the miners, keeping the ledgers secure.

14:09.680 --> 14:11.420
They have to pay the exchange operator.

14:11.840 --> 14:17.540
The miners use those fees to pay their external electricity bills and buy hardware from external

14:17.540 --> 14:18.220
manufacturers.

14:18.220 --> 14:20.620
So cash is physically leaving the room?

14:21.300 --> 14:21.860
Continuously.

14:22.600 --> 14:27.520
Let's say over a month, $200 leaves the room to pay for electricity and exchange fees.

14:27.820 --> 14:31.420
The total amount of real cash left in the room is now $800.

14:31.940 --> 14:33.560
Right, because $200 is just gone.

14:33.560 --> 14:39.120
Yeah, it does not matter if the tokens inside the room are currently valued at a million

14:39.120 --> 14:40.700
dollars based on the last trade.

14:41.380 --> 14:47.140
The aggregate net cash position of the 10 people in that room is negative $200 from their initial

14:47.140 --> 14:47.680
deposits.

14:48.120 --> 14:52.900
The internal trading cannot possibly create a cash surplus for the group as a whole.

14:53.580 --> 14:55.320
But people do make money.

14:55.320 --> 15:01.840
Like, if I deposit real money, buy a token, the price goes up a thousand percent, and I

15:01.840 --> 15:04.380
sell it, I walk away with a massive profit.

15:05.120 --> 15:06.980
I feel like I generated wealth.

15:07.740 --> 15:09.620
You did secure a profit for yourself.

15:09.880 --> 15:12.380
But the mechanism of that profit is what matters.

15:12.920 --> 15:15.720
Where did the cash for your profit actually come from?

15:16.360 --> 15:18.640
Well, it had to come from someone else in the room.

15:19.240 --> 15:21.860
It came directly from another participant's deposit.

15:21.860 --> 15:27.320
The system itself does not generate cash yields from external economic production the way

15:27.320 --> 15:30.600
a traditional factory or a commercial farm does.

15:31.900 --> 15:36.280
A farm takes sunlight and soil, grows wheat, sells it to the outside world, and brings new

15:36.280 --> 15:38.320
cash into the business to pay its investors.

15:38.920 --> 15:40.780
A token ledger does not do that.

15:41.060 --> 15:46.460
It is entirely dependent on new entrants walking into the room and depositing fresh cash to serve

15:46.460 --> 15:49.160
as the exit liquidity for the earlier entrants.

15:49.160 --> 15:53.000
So what you're describing, it sounds literally like a lottery.

15:53.680 --> 15:59.340
People put money into a pool, the house takes a cut for operating the servers, and a few

15:59.340 --> 16:01.640
lucky winners take home the rest of the deposits.

16:02.100 --> 16:07.260
Ah, the author explicitly warns against calling it a lottery, actually, because that misses a

16:07.260 --> 16:10.180
crucial economic nuance about how the winners are selected.

16:10.520 --> 16:11.280
Oh, really?

16:11.600 --> 16:11.860
Okay.

16:12.580 --> 16:13.680
Think about a state lottery.

16:14.400 --> 16:18.080
It is a game of chance with a published, transparent house edge.

16:18.080 --> 16:23.460
The operator tells you up front, we are keeping 40% of the ticket sales for state schools,

16:23.680 --> 16:26.040
and the remaining 60% is the prize pool.

16:26.400 --> 16:27.200
Yeah, that's clear.

16:28.140 --> 16:32.300
It is transparently a negative expectation game based entirely on random chance.

16:32.880 --> 16:35.900
Every ticket has the exact same statistical odds of winning.

16:36.340 --> 16:38.460
But a speculative token market is different.

16:39.180 --> 16:42.160
There is no set published operator edge.

16:42.160 --> 16:47.260
The expected returns for the group as a whole are still negative because of the constant,

16:47.440 --> 16:52.000
relentless drain of resource costs, the electricity and hardware draining cash from the room.

16:52.500 --> 16:56.040
But the winners in a token market aren't determined by random chance.

16:56.360 --> 16:57.260
What determines it then?

16:57.620 --> 17:00.640
The system selects winners based on timing and information.

17:00.640 --> 17:03.500
The early sellers are funded by the late buyers.

17:04.500 --> 17:09.560
And the data the paper cites to back up this mathematical identity is pretty stark.

17:10.100 --> 17:16.180
They reference a February 2023 bulletin from the Bank for International Settlements, or the BIS.

17:16.760 --> 17:22.020
Yes, and the BIS study provides a massive empirical view of this exact phenomenon.

17:22.020 --> 17:30.380
So they looked at retail users across over 200 different crypto exchange apps between 2015 and the end of 2022.

17:30.940 --> 17:35.520
And the bulletin found that a majority of retail users actually lost money.

17:35.760 --> 17:36.440
A vast majority.

17:36.820 --> 17:39.260
Yeah, and it happened in a very specific pattern,

17:39.720 --> 17:44.380
especially around the major collapses of ecosystems like Terraluna and FTX.

17:44.380 --> 17:50.080
What the data showed was that the larger, more sophisticated holders, the early entrants,

17:50.540 --> 17:53.200
sold off their tokens and extracted real cash,

17:53.560 --> 17:57.660
while the smaller retail holders bought in right as things were crashing.

17:57.660 --> 18:01.940
The data perfectly illustrates the cash pool identity we just discussed.

18:02.540 --> 18:06.380
The massive gains of the sophisticated traders who successfully exited the room

18:06.380 --> 18:11.200
were directly funded by the fresh deposits of the retail traders who entered the room late.

18:11.840 --> 18:13.080
The money didn't evaporate.

18:13.080 --> 18:16.860
It was redistributed from the latecomers to the early adopters,

18:17.400 --> 18:19.500
minus the heavy fees paid to the miners.

18:20.400 --> 18:22.060
And if you're listening and thinking,

18:22.260 --> 18:23.820
well, I just need to be early.

18:24.280 --> 18:26.420
I need to follow the volume and the activity.

18:27.520 --> 18:29.040
It gets even more concerning.

18:30.000 --> 18:33.240
The paper cites a study by Cong, Li, Tang, and Yang

18:33.240 --> 18:37.300
regarding how that trading volume is actually generated in the first place.

18:37.620 --> 18:41.500
This is where the informational asymmetry becomes highly predatory.

18:41.500 --> 18:45.860
the Cong study developed robust statistical tests for fabricated trades,

18:45.920 --> 18:47.780
what the industry calls wash trading.

18:48.180 --> 18:48.200
Okay.

18:48.340 --> 18:53.420
Wash trading is when an entity simultaneously buys and sells the same asset to itself, right?

18:53.520 --> 18:53.800
Yes.

18:54.460 --> 18:58.000
You program a bot to buy a token from your left hand and sell it to your right hand

18:58.000 --> 19:00.560
over and over thousands of times a minute.

19:00.560 --> 19:03.360
And the Cong study found that on unregulated exchanges,

19:03.780 --> 19:07.180
over 70% of reported volume is completely fabricated.

19:07.340 --> 19:08.220
Over 70%.

19:08.220 --> 19:09.080
It's a huge number.

19:09.340 --> 19:13.540
The overwhelming majority of the activity you see on the screen is just bots trading back and forth

19:13.540 --> 19:17.160
to make the market look incredibly active, highly liquid, and popular.

19:17.380 --> 19:20.620
And the mechanism there is psychological as much as it is financial.

19:21.060 --> 19:25.260
That manufactured illusion of activity serves a singular necessary purpose.

19:25.700 --> 19:27.920
It attracts new entrants.

19:27.920 --> 19:29.300
Oh, back to the closed room.

19:29.500 --> 19:29.880
Exactly.

19:30.160 --> 19:34.500
To get your profit out, you need new people to walk into the room with fresh cash.

19:35.060 --> 19:39.420
Wash trading is the flashing neon sign outside the room claiming it's the hottest party in town.

19:39.880 --> 19:41.720
The new entrants see the fake volume.

19:42.100 --> 19:47.320
They deposit fresh cash, which provides the exact mechanism that allows the earlier holders

19:47.320 --> 19:49.800
to convert their tokens into real money and exit.

19:50.300 --> 19:55.840
It is like a nightclub promoter putting up fake sold-out signs and paying people to stand in a line down the block

19:55.840 --> 20:02.960
just to make the club look justified so they can charge a massive cover fee to the unsuspecting people who finally get to the door.

20:03.940 --> 20:04.940
That's a perfect analogy.

20:05.340 --> 20:08.880
But this brings up a huge defense that the crypto industry always uses.

20:09.340 --> 20:14.560
Since you mentioned that winners rely on information and timing, we have to talk about price discovery.

20:14.560 --> 20:20.060
Yes, price discovery is one of the foundational concepts of traditional financial markets.

20:20.480 --> 20:20.580
Right.

20:20.980 --> 20:25.880
Wall Street analysts are always saying the market is valuable because it figures out the true price of things.

20:26.120 --> 20:32.320
Even if there's speculation, the speculation produces information, and that information is a service to society.

20:32.840 --> 20:35.040
So how does the paper deal with that defense?

20:35.620 --> 20:40.680
Well, Phloxam examines this through the lens of Hirschleifer's 1971 theory of information.

20:40.680 --> 20:45.160
The defense of price discovery is absolutely sound where it applies.

20:45.860 --> 20:50.300
But Hirschleifer's theory states that price discovery is only socially valuable

20:50.300 --> 20:57.480
if the newly discovered price improves a decision about real-world production, resource allocation, or risk sharing.

20:58.220 --> 21:00.240
Let's use a TradFi example to see how that works.

21:01.220 --> 21:05.360
Imagine a publicly traded company that manufactures solar panels.

21:05.360 --> 21:10.260
Okay, so if the stock price of that solar panel manufacturer suddenly goes up

21:10.260 --> 21:14.080
because traders discover new information about a breakthrough in panel efficiency,

21:14.360 --> 21:17.940
that rising price is highly valuable to the outside world.

21:18.160 --> 21:19.060
Because it sends a signal.

21:19.280 --> 21:20.640
It sends a massive signal.

21:21.220 --> 21:25.080
It tells the company's board of directors that they should allocate capital to build a new factory.

21:25.520 --> 21:28.940
It tells banks and lenders that it is safe to give the company a loan.

21:29.480 --> 21:32.720
It tells rival energy companies how to adjust their physical strategies.

21:32.720 --> 21:36.040
The stock market in this case is an open market.

21:36.460 --> 21:37.200
Open market.

21:37.980 --> 21:41.800
Meaning the information discovered by the traders directs physical resources,

21:42.280 --> 21:45.540
steel, concrete, labor, to where society most demands them.

21:45.760 --> 21:46.220
Exactly.

21:46.700 --> 21:50.660
But now let's apply Proposition 3 from the text to a digital token.

21:50.980 --> 21:54.300
We are talking about a token that has no claim on cash flows,

21:54.560 --> 21:58.660
no right to a physical service, no legal obligation from any counterparty.

21:58.700 --> 22:00.760
It just exists on the ledger.

22:00.760 --> 22:03.040
And the text calls this a closed market.

22:03.300 --> 22:03.580
Yes.

22:04.400 --> 22:06.220
The distinction is absolute.

22:06.640 --> 22:12.160
If traders spend millions of dollars building algorithms to discover the perfect price of a closed market token,

22:12.720 --> 22:14.800
what does that price signal to the outside world?

22:15.140 --> 22:17.420
It doesn't tell anyone how to build a better factory.

22:17.780 --> 22:19.880
It doesn't guide the allocation of physical goods.

22:20.480 --> 22:26.360
The token's price is statistically independent of any state that matters to real world production.

22:26.360 --> 22:31.400
So according to the mathematical models of Grossman, Stiglitz, and Kyle that the author cites,

22:32.020 --> 22:37.480
the resources spent acquiring information just to trade that token are just wasted.

22:37.840 --> 22:38.480
Entirely.

22:38.640 --> 22:44.020
The brilliant quants, the dedicated fiber optic server connections, the thousands of human hours,

22:44.180 --> 22:47.240
all of it is a complete deadweight loss to society.

22:47.240 --> 22:48.660
Think of it like this.

22:49.120 --> 22:55.380
Imagine spending millions of dollars running supercomputers and hiring the smartest mathematicians in the world

22:55.380 --> 23:02.140
to discover the absolute optimal, mathematically perfect strategy for a multiplayer video game.

23:02.660 --> 23:05.860
You've discovered incredible, highly complex information.

23:05.860 --> 23:06.720
You have.

23:07.100 --> 23:09.560
But the video game does not connect to the real world.

23:10.260 --> 23:16.320
The information you discovered changes who holds the high score and maybe who holds the gold coins inside the game,

23:16.440 --> 23:22.680
but it does absolutely nothing to increase the quantity of food, housing, or energy available outside the game.

23:23.320 --> 23:25.060
The price discovery leads nowhere.

23:25.940 --> 23:30.980
I hear that, but, you know, if you're listening to this and you remember the news from 2022 and early 2023,

23:31.820 --> 23:34.720
you might be kind of screaming at the deep dive right now.

23:34.720 --> 23:35.460
Oh, I'm sure.

23:35.460 --> 23:41.740
Because I distinctly remember that when the crypto market crashed, when FTX and others went down,

23:42.480 --> 23:44.040
the whole banking system shook.

23:44.920 --> 23:49.800
We had actual, regulated, real-world banks fail in March of 2023.

23:50.820 --> 23:54.800
So doesn't that prove that the crypto market is connected to the real world?

23:54.880 --> 23:57.020
Doesn't that mean it is an open market?

23:57.300 --> 24:00.740
That is a very common and, frankly, very dangerous conflation.

24:01.260 --> 24:04.660
Flyxion draws a critical distinction here that we really have to understand.

24:04.660 --> 24:09.600
Financial interconnectedness is not the same thing as informational openness.

24:10.060 --> 24:10.620
Break that down.

24:10.940 --> 24:16.480
How can a market be connected enough to crash a bank but still be considered closed and useless?

24:16.480 --> 24:21.820
If a regulated bank decides to hold billions of dollars in cash deposits for a crypto exchange,

24:22.280 --> 24:30.200
or if wealthy token holders suffer a massive wealth effect from a market crash and suddenly stop buying real estate or cars in the real economy,

24:30.760 --> 24:32.260
that is a channel of propagation.

24:32.520 --> 24:33.320
It is a shockwave.

24:33.760 --> 24:34.840
It's the blast radius.

24:34.840 --> 24:35.520
Exactly.

24:36.160 --> 24:42.400
A market can be completely informationally useless, meaning its prices provide no signal to help society allocate resources,

24:42.680 --> 24:48.720
but it can still transmit devastating financial shockwaves through its counterparties because of how the plumbing is hooked up.

24:49.280 --> 24:52.520
Harmful exposure is a matter of financial cost and interconnectedness.

24:52.620 --> 24:54.800
It is not a measure of beneficial information.

24:54.800 --> 25:01.800
So what you're telling me is that a system can be a closed loop informationally, producing absolutely nothing of real-world value,

25:02.320 --> 25:07.600
while still being financially connected enough to do massive collateral damage to the real economy when it fails.

25:07.880 --> 25:09.720
That is exactly what the math shows.

25:09.980 --> 25:11.900
That is a soberingly grim reality.

25:12.540 --> 25:16.300
And it forces us to ask the next logical evolutionary question.

25:16.300 --> 25:24.580
If this market is a closed loop and it is not rewarding real-world productivity, what exactly is the system evolving to reward?

25:25.000 --> 25:25.120
Right.

25:25.560 --> 25:32.020
And this brings us to the physics in the hardware of the network, which is arguably the most tangible part of the paper.

25:32.900 --> 25:36.420
The text introduces Proposition 4, Selection Neutrality.

25:37.040 --> 25:44.340
And the author uses something called a Gibbs form distribution to prove a mathematical point about how these systems evolve over time.

25:44.340 --> 25:51.020
Yeah, the math here relies on statistical mechanics, but we can understand the mechanism without getting bogged down in the formulas.

25:51.820 --> 25:58.200
A Gibbs distribution basically describes the probability of a system being in a certain state based on its energy.

25:59.040 --> 26:06.400
In this context, Flyxion uses it to model how capital and resources flow into different tokens based on their, quote, fitness.

26:06.400 --> 26:16.140
And in a market, the ultimate fitness trait, the thing that attracts capital and ensures survival, is price appreciation, going up in value.

26:16.500 --> 26:16.760
Right.

26:17.440 --> 26:25.180
The author proves mathematically that if the fitness of a token, its price, is statistically independent of its productivity in the real world,

26:25.360 --> 26:29.040
the system will never naturally select for productive configurations.

26:29.420 --> 26:30.860
Because the market just doesn't care.

26:30.860 --> 26:37.800
The update rules of the network, the code that runs the ledger, never explicitly reference real-world productivity.

26:38.720 --> 26:44.000
And if the price goes up regardless of whether the token is useful, the market will just chase the price.

26:44.760 --> 26:45.220
Precisely.

26:45.520 --> 26:55.100
And because of the way proof-of-work mining operates, this mathematical neutrality leads directly to a massive real-world physical problem, which is rent dissipation.

26:55.100 --> 27:00.840
This concept relies on classic economic theories from Tulloch and Posner regarding contests.

27:01.440 --> 27:11.120
If you have free entry into a competition for a prize of a fixed value, the entrants will spend resources competing for that prize right up until the marginal return on spending is zero.

27:11.940 --> 27:13.260
Let's apply that to Bitcoin mining.

27:13.260 --> 27:19.020
Okay, the reward for adding a block of transactions to the Bitcoin ledger is paid in the token itself.

27:19.560 --> 27:27.760
Because anyone in the world can buy a computer and join the network, free entry, miners will spend money on electricity and hardware to compete for that block reward.

27:28.480 --> 27:31.000
And the arms race mechanism is actually baked into the code.

27:31.700 --> 27:36.440
When the token price goes up, the block reward is suddenly worth a lot more real-world cash.

27:36.780 --> 27:37.040
Yes.

27:37.040 --> 27:41.800
Because the prize is bigger, new miners plug in more machines to try and win it.

27:42.060 --> 27:45.320
But the network is programmed to only release one block every 10 minutes.

27:45.860 --> 27:52.640
So, to keep the system stable despite all this new computing power, the algorithm artificially makes the math puzzle harder.

27:53.280 --> 27:54.940
That is the rent dissipation.

27:54.940 --> 28:01.640
The whole network is now burning twice the electricity and running twice as many machines to process the exact same number of transactions.

28:01.640 --> 28:09.060
The energy spending tracks the monetary value of the reward, not the usefulness of the payments the network is settling.

28:10.080 --> 28:12.220
The scale of this arms race is staggering.

28:13.160 --> 28:18.320
The paper references the Cambridge Center for Alternative Finance's Electricity Consumption Index.

28:18.960 --> 28:26.080
They estimated Bitcoin's annual energy use in a band ranging from 21 all the way up to 146 terawatt hours.

28:26.460 --> 28:28.380
That's a massive amount of power.

28:28.380 --> 28:35.300
That is the energy consumption of a medium-sized country dedicated entirely to a closed-loop guessing game.

28:35.880 --> 28:36.160
It is.

28:36.700 --> 28:37.860
But we must be rigorous here.

28:38.100 --> 28:42.000
If you follow this space, you know that the industry has addressed the energy issue.

28:42.160 --> 28:43.300
We have to talk about Ethereum.

28:43.740 --> 28:44.060
Right.

28:44.460 --> 28:45.020
The merge.

28:45.980 --> 28:53.840
In September of 2022, the Ethereum network moved from a proof-of-work system, which requires all that electricity, to a proof-of-stake system, which does not.

28:53.960 --> 28:54.200
Yes.

28:54.200 --> 29:01.420
And the Ethereum Foundation reported that this single update reduced the network's energy consumption by 99.95%.

29:01.420 --> 29:08.920
So I have to ask, if Ethereum fixed the energy problem, doesn't that completely dismantle Flyxion's whole critique about resource waste?

29:09.260 --> 29:11.720
It is a vital question, but the answer is no.

29:12.120 --> 29:18.140
In fact, if you look at the math, Flyxion points out that the Ethereum merge actually strengthens the core thesis of the paper.

29:18.140 --> 29:22.480
Wait, how does removing the energy waste strengthen a paper about waste?

29:23.020 --> 29:26.940
Because it proves that the energy problem was merely a consensus problem.

29:27.300 --> 29:30.780
It was just a highly inefficient way to get the computers to agree.

29:31.240 --> 29:32.700
It was not a ledger problem.

29:33.240 --> 29:35.380
Fixing the energy consumption is fantastic.

29:35.560 --> 29:38.840
It drastically lowers the resource cost in our net work reduction equation.

29:38.840 --> 29:44.640
But changing to proof-of-stake does absolutely nothing to fix the closed cash pool identity.

29:45.020 --> 29:47.820
It does nothing to fix the closed-loop information problem.

29:48.200 --> 29:52.260
It does not suddenly make a token represent a claim on real-world production.

29:53.120 --> 29:57.340
So it just makes a useless closed-loop system slightly cheaper to run.

29:57.640 --> 29:58.480
Basically, yeah.

29:58.480 --> 30:03.940
It's like taking the massive gas-guzzling engine out of a car that is just spinning its wheels in the mud

30:03.940 --> 30:08.320
and replacing it with an incredibly sleek, energy-efficient electric motor.

30:09.060 --> 30:10.380
It is much greener, yes.

30:10.960 --> 30:13.200
But you are still stuck in the mud.

30:13.280 --> 30:14.280
You still aren't going anywhere.

30:14.580 --> 30:15.520
That's a great way to put it.

30:15.940 --> 30:19.160
And we cannot ignore that energy is not the only material cost.

30:19.400 --> 30:22.060
The hardware itself is a massive externality.

30:22.740 --> 30:26.380
The paper cites a study by DeVries and Stoll regarding electronic waste.

30:26.380 --> 30:30.000
This was the part of the paper that genuinely shocked me.

30:30.380 --> 30:30.960
E-waste.

30:31.380 --> 30:33.720
They estimated that, as of May 2021,

30:34.140 --> 30:40.560
Bitcoin mining alone generated roughly 30,700 metric tons of electronic waste annually.

30:41.180 --> 30:42.140
To put that in perspective,

30:42.460 --> 30:46.800
that is comparable to the entire small information technology equipment waste

30:46.800 --> 30:48.280
of the country of the Netherlands.

30:48.960 --> 30:51.000
Why is there so much physical garbage, though?

30:51.520 --> 30:53.140
Because of the arms race we discussed.

30:53.140 --> 30:58.060
The highly specialized mining machines, called ASICs, can only do one thing,

30:58.340 --> 30:59.880
which is guess the math puzzle.

31:00.240 --> 31:00.300
Right.

31:00.400 --> 31:01.420
They aren't normal computers.

31:01.620 --> 31:01.800
No.

31:02.220 --> 31:05.780
And because the network constantly gets harder as new, faster machines are built,

31:06.100 --> 31:08.380
older machines become unprofitable very quickly.

31:08.940 --> 31:13.760
The paper notes their average competitive device lifetime is roughly 1.25 years.

31:14.420 --> 31:16.180
After that, they are completely obsolete.

31:16.580 --> 31:19.440
They cannot be repurposed to render video or run AI.

31:19.760 --> 31:21.520
They just become toxic junk.

31:21.520 --> 31:26.360
And tying this all the way back to our very first mathematical definition of productivity,

31:27.160 --> 31:29.740
the global domain, Omega Global.

31:30.840 --> 31:35.020
Who bears the cost of those 30,700 tons of toxic e-waste?

31:35.580 --> 31:36.980
Not the traders making the profits.

31:37.720 --> 31:38.980
The global domain bears it.

31:39.300 --> 31:40.700
The people living near the landfills.

31:41.140 --> 31:44.300
The communities dealing with heavy metal runoff into their water tables.

31:44.300 --> 31:48.940
The system is shifting its true physical costs onto non-participants.

31:49.740 --> 31:53.400
Which is why Flyxion insists that any honest accounting of the technology's value

31:53.400 --> 31:55.480
has to include these externalities.

31:55.840 --> 31:58.120
You can't just look at the perfectly secure vault.

31:58.480 --> 32:01.100
You have to look at the smog pouring out of the back of the building.

32:01.100 --> 32:05.740
We have spent a lot of time on the intense mathematical theories,

32:05.960 --> 32:09.140
the cash pools, the Gibbs distribution, the externalities.

32:09.620 --> 32:12.280
But Flyxion does not leave this in the realm of theory.

32:12.760 --> 32:17.420
The paper deliberately grounds these equations by examining the messy reality of recent history.

32:17.420 --> 32:24.400
Yes. The paper looks at four specific case studies highlighted in a 2026 CBC documentary called

32:24.400 --> 32:27.620
Everyone is Lying to You for Money, directed by Ben McKenzie,

32:27.840 --> 32:31.020
as well as the surrounding court reports and historical data.

32:31.820 --> 32:36.840
And the author uses these four cases not as definitive statistical proof of the entire industry,

32:37.000 --> 32:42.840
but as illustrative examples of the mathematical premises we just discussed playing out in the real world.

32:43.300 --> 32:46.440
Now, as your host, I have to enforce a strict rule here.

32:46.440 --> 32:51.820
If you are listening, please know that we are going to impartially report on the historical facts,

32:52.300 --> 32:56.780
the government data, and the court convictions, precisely as they are presented in the text.

32:57.460 --> 32:58.620
It's important to be clear on that.

32:58.960 --> 33:00.800
Yes, we are not taking a political stance,

33:01.020 --> 33:05.860
and we are not endorsing the documentary's broader ideological or political claims.

33:05.940 --> 33:11.220
We are simply conveying the case studies the author uses to map the math to reality.

33:11.480 --> 33:13.940
That is the intellectually honest approach.

33:13.940 --> 33:16.660
Let's look at the first case study, which is El Salvador.

33:17.160 --> 33:22.360
So in 2021, El Salvador made global headlines by adopting Bitcoin as legal tender.

33:22.800 --> 33:29.280
The primary headline use case, the bundle of needs we talked about earlier, was remittances.

33:29.840 --> 33:30.100
Right.

33:30.480 --> 33:32.300
The stated goal was to provide a cheaper,

33:32.540 --> 33:36.480
faster way for Salvadoran workers living abroad to send money home to their families.

33:36.480 --> 33:39.480
This is a perfect example of how to apply the framework.

33:40.000 --> 33:43.020
Remittances represent a legitimate, independent human need.

33:43.120 --> 33:45.980
People need to send money home, whether crypto exists or not.

33:46.920 --> 33:51.840
The counterfactual cost is whatever traditional services like Western Union or international bank

33:51.840 --> 33:53.440
wires charge, which can be quite high.

33:54.260 --> 33:57.720
If the digital ledger could facilitate those transfers cheaper and faster,

33:57.960 --> 34:00.360
it would generate a positive net work reduction.

34:00.760 --> 34:02.180
It would be mathematically productive.

34:02.180 --> 34:05.300
But the reality on the ground was drastically different.

34:06.080 --> 34:09.860
The documentary reports that according to the government's own central bank figures,

34:10.580 --> 34:14.840
only about 1.6% of remittances were actually paid in Bitcoin.

34:15.260 --> 34:16.180
Just 1.6%.

34:17.020 --> 34:17.240
Yeah.

34:17.680 --> 34:22.620
And there is an interview in the film where Sam Bankman freed before the collapse of his empire

34:22.620 --> 34:26.760
actually admitted that the remittance use case wasn't really working.

34:26.760 --> 34:32.460
He acknowledged that the vast majority of people were just using it as a speculative financial asset,

34:32.660 --> 34:33.640
not a payment vehicle.

34:34.300 --> 34:36.120
Which maps directly to our equations.

34:37.400 --> 34:41.380
1.6% uptake does not mean the system is completely empty.

34:41.740 --> 34:46.900
Some people did use it, but it severely limits the scale of demonstrated provision.

34:47.660 --> 34:51.400
It shows that the technology struggled to beat the counterfactual cost,

34:51.400 --> 34:58.600
the ease of use, the trust, the stability of existing accessible networks that people already rely on.

34:59.040 --> 35:00.620
And the externalities were severe.

35:01.480 --> 35:05.380
The film noted that a planned infrastructure project for a Bitcoin city

35:05.380 --> 35:07.920
threatened to displace a local fishing community,

35:08.220 --> 35:10.460
offering them a fraction of their land's actual value.

35:11.060 --> 35:13.720
This is a textbook manifestation of Omega Global.

35:14.200 --> 35:19.640
The people potentially losing their land and their livelihood are bearing the costs of an unproductive system.

35:19.640 --> 35:23.380
They are the non-participants absorbing the negative value.

35:24.020 --> 35:26.080
Let's move to the second case, which is Celsius.

35:27.240 --> 35:30.040
Celsius was a platform marketed to retail investors,

35:30.520 --> 35:33.360
essentially as a high-yield replacement for traditional banks.

35:34.240 --> 35:40.980
In 2024, the former CEO, Alex Mashinsky, pleaded guilty to fraud and manipulating the token price.

35:41.540 --> 35:44.640
And in 2025, he was sentenced to 12 years in prison.

35:44.640 --> 35:50.060
The reporting noted that customers lost over $5 billion when the platform collapsed,

35:50.620 --> 35:54.780
but Flyxion points to a very specific structural admission regarding Celsius.

35:55.360 --> 35:58.940
There is an incredible moment from the documentary cited in the text.

35:59.700 --> 36:04.260
Mashinsky, in an audio-only recording captured after he thought the cameras were off,

36:04.920 --> 36:09.300
estimated that maybe 10 to 15 percent of the money in the crypto system was real money,

36:09.600 --> 36:10.800
that it had actually been deposited.

36:10.800 --> 36:14.580
And the other 85 to 90 percent was just bubble.

36:15.440 --> 36:20.820
Now, the paper is careful to note that this is an offhand estimate from an interested party caught on tape

36:20.820 --> 36:23.140
and is not peer-reviewed scientific data.

36:23.440 --> 36:27.560
But look at how perfectly the shape of his estimate matches Proposition 2,

36:27.820 --> 36:29.700
the cash pool identity we discussed earlier.

36:29.860 --> 36:30.620
The closed room.

36:30.760 --> 36:31.120
Exactly.

36:31.620 --> 36:34.480
The total real cash that can be withdrawn from the system

36:34.480 --> 36:38.660
is strictly bounded by the hard cash that actually walked into the room.

36:38.660 --> 36:43.820
If only 15 percent of the ecosystem's perceived value is backed by real cash deposits,

36:44.140 --> 36:46.920
the system can only ever pay out that 15 percent.

36:47.340 --> 36:47.420
Right.

36:47.840 --> 36:51.260
The other 85 percent is a fabricated closed loop,

36:51.540 --> 36:54.820
an illusion of wealth created by internal trading and token inflation.

36:55.380 --> 37:00.100
When the music stops, the 85 percent vanishes because it never existed in the real economy.

37:00.360 --> 37:02.240
That is a chilling realization.

37:02.720 --> 37:05.980
When you tie the math to the actual victims who lost their life savings.

37:05.980 --> 37:10.340
Which brings us to the third case study, FTX.

37:10.740 --> 37:11.020
Right.

37:11.260 --> 37:14.960
Sam Bankman-Fried was sentenced to 25 years in prison in 2024

37:14.960 --> 37:18.860
following the catastrophic collapse of the FTX exchange.

37:19.060 --> 37:23.520
But the core of this case, for the paper's purposes, is not the fraud itself.

37:24.000 --> 37:28.540
It is how the collapse highlights the distinction we made earlier about contagion.

37:28.540 --> 37:33.400
Yeah, the film highlights how the FTX collapse and the broader crypto crash of late 2022

37:33.400 --> 37:39.100
was deeply intertwined with the failure of three U.S. banks in March of 2023.

37:39.860 --> 37:45.780
Silvergate, Silicon Valley Bank, and Signature Bank all failed or were taken over in rapid succession.

37:46.300 --> 37:51.780
This perfectly proves the difference between informational openness and financial interconnectedness.

37:51.780 --> 37:57.940
The prices of the tokens trading on FTX did not provide any useful information to guide the broader economy.

37:58.440 --> 38:00.580
But the financial architecture was connected.

38:00.840 --> 38:01.740
Deeply connected.

38:02.140 --> 38:07.920
The plumbing was connected to these regulated banks enough to transmit a massive destructive shockwave.

38:08.140 --> 38:11.340
The risk was exported to the traditional financial system.

38:11.720 --> 38:14.260
And the final case study focuses on wash trading.

38:14.260 --> 38:22.100
The documentary features an interviewee who boldly claims that 90 to 95 percent of all crypto trading volume is completely fake.

38:22.780 --> 38:26.020
But the author of our paper actually pushes back on that specific number.

38:26.420 --> 38:27.020
Why push back?

38:27.320 --> 38:29.760
A higher number makes the author's argument look stronger.

38:30.220 --> 38:32.660
Because Flyxion demands rigorous evidence.

38:32.980 --> 38:36.260
The paper advises sticking to the peer-reviewed estimate we discussed earlier,

38:36.400 --> 38:42.620
the Cong et al. study showing about 70 percent fabricated volume on unregulated exchanges.

38:42.620 --> 38:49.620
The point the author makes is that you do not need the exaggerated, dramatic 95 percent number from a documentary

38:49.620 --> 38:52.520
to make the structural mathematical argument.

38:53.140 --> 38:55.520
70 percent fabricated volume is more than enough.

38:55.760 --> 38:58.640
It is more than enough to prove that the baseline for the Gibbs distribution,

38:58.860 --> 39:04.260
the fitness landscape that attracts capital and engineering talent, is fundamentally corrupted.

39:04.940 --> 39:10.480
The system is evolving to reward the illusion of activity, not the provision of real work.

39:10.480 --> 39:15.860
Okay, so we have mathematically established that these closed-loop systems drain real resources,

39:16.120 --> 39:20.420
they struggle to produce real-world value, and they inevitably collapse or shut down.

39:20.920 --> 39:25.260
But if you're listening, you might be wondering, what happens when the music finally stops?

39:25.700 --> 39:26.760
That's the tragic part.

39:26.980 --> 39:27.320
It is.

39:27.980 --> 39:32.560
This brings us to what is arguably the most human part of the paper, the aftermath.

39:33.320 --> 39:37.140
When these ventures inevitably fail, who is left holding the back?

39:37.140 --> 39:42.160
The text asks a profound structural question about what survives an exit.

39:42.340 --> 39:45.480
Because remember, the ledger itself might keep perfect records.

39:46.060 --> 39:49.060
The blockchain doesn't turn off just because a company goes bankrupt.

39:49.460 --> 39:55.040
But a perfect mathematical record of who owes what does not actually pay people back.

39:55.560 --> 40:00.020
To address this structural flaw, the author proposes a concept called unbundling.

40:00.020 --> 40:07.180
They argue that a single digital token typically forces three very different functions together into one tradable asset.

40:07.960 --> 40:14.220
It acts as a record of work, a medium of payment, and a source of authority or governance.

40:14.920 --> 40:18.780
And when you fuse those three distinct functions into a single tradable token,

40:19.260 --> 40:23.060
the speculative price of the token becomes the only thing that matters.

40:23.780 --> 40:28.600
Early participation in the network converts into perpetual control over the network.

40:28.600 --> 40:35.480
And worse, if an early founder makes a highly profitable exit by dumping all their tokens on retail investors,

40:35.720 --> 40:39.160
they can effectively walk away and the system collapses.

40:39.720 --> 40:42.260
So Flyxion proposes separating them.

40:42.860 --> 40:47.100
Keep the cryptographic record of contributions distinct from the payment mechanism

40:47.100 --> 40:50.480
and separate both of those from the authority to make decisions.

40:50.480 --> 40:56.040
You do not need a public blockchain with a highly volatile, heavily traded speculative token

40:56.040 --> 40:58.760
just to keep a verified log of who did what.

40:59.080 --> 41:03.180
But unbundling the technology doesn't solve the economic fallout of a failure.

41:03.760 --> 41:07.720
And this is where the paper introduces Proposition 5, the math of survival.

41:08.680 --> 41:14.940
This looks at the brutal asymmetry between a diversified investor and a local dependent.

41:14.940 --> 41:20.640
Let's break down the math of this asymmetry because it explains why so much money continues to flow into systems

41:20.640 --> 41:22.420
that fail the productivity test.

41:23.120 --> 41:24.960
Imagine a wealthy venture capital investor.

41:25.400 --> 41:27.920
Let's call them the diversified investor.

41:28.260 --> 41:28.300
Okay.

41:28.640 --> 41:35.160
They have a massive pool of capital and they can fund, let's say, 50 different highly speculative token ventures.

41:35.560 --> 41:39.320
This number of ventures is represented by the variable J in the equation.

41:39.320 --> 41:45.500
The investor knows full well that the vast majority of these ventures, maybe 45 out of 50,

41:45.700 --> 41:47.960
are going to completely fail and go to absolutely zero.

41:48.660 --> 41:50.300
But the investor is perfectly fine with that.

41:50.420 --> 41:50.640
Why?

41:51.060 --> 41:54.600
Because the success multiplier, represented by M in the equation,

41:55.220 --> 41:59.260
on the 5% that actually succeed is so astronomically large

41:59.260 --> 42:02.780
that it more than covers the losses of the 45 that failed.

42:03.160 --> 42:05.980
The investor's aggregate portfolio is highly profitable.

42:05.980 --> 42:10.880
The investor's basically playing roulette, but they have chips spread across 50 different tables.

42:11.280 --> 42:15.800
If 49 tables lose, the one table that hits 35 to 1 pays for the whole night.

42:16.160 --> 42:18.140
They are insulated by their diversification.

42:18.620 --> 42:21.120
But what about the employee of one of those failed companies?

42:21.820 --> 42:25.920
What about the local supplier who sold them thousands of server racks?

42:26.580 --> 42:28.820
What about the retail customer in a developing country

42:28.820 --> 42:33.200
whose life savings were locked on the platform when withdrawals were paused?

42:33.200 --> 42:38.540
The text proves mathematically that the local dependent bears the full variance of the failure.

42:39.160 --> 42:41.500
They are not diversified across 50 ventures.

42:41.760 --> 42:46.420
They suffer the total, unmitigated loss of their specific venture going to zero.

42:46.700 --> 42:50.260
They are tied to just one chair at one roulette table.

42:50.980 --> 42:54.420
When that specific table loses, they are completely wiped out.

42:54.980 --> 43:00.000
The VC investor in Silicon Valley celebrates their aggregate portfolio return,

43:00.000 --> 43:02.900
while the local dependent is financially devastated.

43:03.640 --> 43:04.700
It's a huge structural flaw.

43:05.420 --> 43:11.380
So how does the author propose we fix this fundamental unfairness in the architecture of innovation?

43:12.100 --> 43:17.580
The solution proposed is a mandatory closure reserve, denoted mathematically as K.

43:18.000 --> 43:22.120
This would be a specific, calculated amount of capital placed in an independent,

43:22.800 --> 43:27.660
uncensorable escrow completely outside the control of the token holders or the project founders.

43:27.660 --> 43:33.580
And this reserve, K, pre-funds three very specific elements of a failure, right?

43:34.280 --> 43:34.500
Yes.

43:34.840 --> 43:37.400
First, physical and environmental remediation.

43:37.720 --> 43:40.980
If a venture built a massive mining farm that drains local resources,

43:41.620 --> 43:43.140
K pays to decommission the site.

43:43.760 --> 43:47.860
Clean up the thousands of tons of toxic e-waste and restore the land.

43:48.080 --> 43:50.160
Because you do not get to just declare bankruptcy,

43:50.380 --> 43:54.320
shrug your shoulders and leave a toxic warehouse for the local town's taxpayers to clean up.

43:54.320 --> 43:57.660
Exactly. Which protects the global domain, Omega Global.

43:58.300 --> 43:59.820
Second is archival cost.

44:00.140 --> 44:04.660
This pays to preserve the historical state and the cryptographically verified records of the users.

44:05.240 --> 44:08.600
If the company goes under, the reserve ensures the servers stay on long enough

44:08.600 --> 44:12.640
so users can actually access and verify their claims in bankruptcy court.

44:13.000 --> 44:14.880
And third is transition cost.

44:15.320 --> 44:20.460
This covers the real-world resources needed to move dependents to an alternative arrangement.

44:20.460 --> 44:24.340
Things like employee severance and retraining, relocation costs,

44:24.480 --> 44:27.640
or migrating user data safely to a functioning service.

44:28.060 --> 44:33.940
This is so structurally smart, it forces the venture to internalize the true cost of their own failure.

44:34.600 --> 44:40.000
Under the current paradigm, an investor might fund a highly marginal, totally unproven idea,

44:40.180 --> 44:44.740
because if it fails, the local community and the retail users eat the cleanup cost.

44:44.740 --> 44:50.400
But if the investor has to pre-fund Reserve K out of their own pocket before they can even launch,

44:50.980 --> 44:52.920
suddenly the math of innovation changes.

44:53.120 --> 44:53.500
Exactly.

44:54.000 --> 45:00.760
By requiring K, unprofitable or highly risky ideas that rely on dumping the costs of failure onto dependents

45:00.760 --> 45:03.100
simply will not get funded in the first place.

45:03.640 --> 45:09.060
It changes the threshold of profitability to reflect reality, rather than a subsidized illusion.

45:09.060 --> 45:09.700
All right.

45:10.140 --> 45:13.560
We have built a really rigorous, mathematically backed case here.

45:14.040 --> 45:18.740
We've gone from the core cryptography to the physical e-waste to the VC math.

45:19.220 --> 45:23.320
But to be fair to the ecosystem and to our listeners who might work in this space,

45:23.880 --> 45:28.840
we have to stress test this framework against the most common defenses people make for these technologies.

45:29.680 --> 45:35.300
In Section 8, the paper addresses five core objections, and I want to integrate these organically.

45:35.800 --> 45:37.040
Let's test the framework.

45:37.040 --> 45:38.680
Okay. First objection.

45:39.660 --> 45:42.340
Whenever I talk to my friends who trade tokens, they always say,

45:42.760 --> 45:47.660
the market is valuable because it is highly liquid, providing liquidity is a service in itself.

45:47.740 --> 45:49.740
I can trade in and out 24-7.

45:50.060 --> 45:53.500
The author's reply is grounded in Hirschleifer's theory again.

45:53.800 --> 45:59.040
Liquidity is only a socially useful service if the underlying asset actually does real work.

45:59.140 --> 45:59.260
Right.

45:59.580 --> 46:05.600
If the asset represents a share in a factory, making it easier to buy and sell that share helps allocate capital efficiently.

46:05.600 --> 46:13.640
But liquidating claims on future buyers, just making it mathematically frictionless to buy and sell a token that has no real world link,

46:14.100 --> 46:15.540
is just a service to speculators.

46:16.120 --> 46:19.760
It does not reduce the cost of delivering an independent need to society.

46:20.580 --> 46:22.520
Frictionless gambling is still just gambling.

46:23.120 --> 46:23.260
Okay.

46:23.260 --> 46:24.540
Okay. Second objection.

46:24.940 --> 46:29.560
It acts as a hedge against inflation or a store of value against a corrupt government.

46:30.220 --> 46:37.380
Well, a financial instrument only functions as a true hedge if its returns co-vary inversely with bad real-world states.

46:37.920 --> 46:42.260
Meaning the token reliably goes up in value when the real economy goes down.

46:42.260 --> 46:43.140
Precisely.

46:43.720 --> 46:50.020
But if an asset's price is purely driven by internal flows of attention, social media hype, and speculative wash trading,

46:50.260 --> 46:52.000
it is not a reliable hedge.

46:52.280 --> 46:53.980
It is uncorrelated chaos.

46:54.380 --> 46:57.800
It only provides that hedging service, incidentally, if at all.

46:58.140 --> 47:03.840
And history shows these assets often crash at the exact moment liquidity tightens in the broader economy.

47:04.380 --> 47:05.180
Third objection.

47:05.180 --> 47:08.220
Look, maybe it's not a new financial system.

47:08.520 --> 47:10.140
Maybe people just enjoy trading it.

47:10.540 --> 47:11.360
It's entertainment.

47:11.520 --> 47:12.040
It's a gain.

47:12.700 --> 47:13.860
And entertainment has value.

47:14.520 --> 47:17.860
What is fascinating is that the author actually agrees with this premise.

47:18.180 --> 47:19.920
Entertainment is a real human need.

47:20.320 --> 47:24.880
But if it is entertainment, we must judge it and regulate it exactly like gambling.

47:25.400 --> 47:26.340
Which means transparency.

47:27.060 --> 47:27.300
Yes.

47:27.700 --> 47:30.620
We should demand disclosure of the negative expected odds.

47:31.020 --> 47:32.280
We should treat it like a casino.

47:32.280 --> 47:38.700
We should not misclassify a digital casino as technological progress or economic empowerment

47:38.700 --> 47:42.040
and assess it under the criteria of economic productivity.

47:42.340 --> 47:45.900
If it's a game, call it a game and protect the players accordingly.

47:46.380 --> 47:48.360
That is a very sharp distinction.

47:48.760 --> 47:49.800
Okay, the fourth objection.

47:50.320 --> 47:55.640
And I really want to zero in on this one because it feels like a massive pivot that reframes the entire paper.

47:56.100 --> 47:57.880
The objection is, wait a minute.

47:57.880 --> 48:03.060
This entire framework, the closure test, the net work reduction, the cash pool identity,

48:03.720 --> 48:08.180
if you apply this fairly, it condemns a huge portion of traditional finance too.

48:08.920 --> 48:14.120
Is this whole paper actually a weapon that can be aimed at Wall Street just as easily as it can be aimed at crypto?

48:14.520 --> 48:17.340
The author emphatically affirms this.

48:17.680 --> 48:17.940
Yes.

48:18.240 --> 48:21.680
It condemns the parts of traditional finance that fail the closure test.

48:22.060 --> 48:26.300
And that is a feature of a good mathematically sound theory, not a bug.

48:26.300 --> 48:28.520
The math doesn't care about your pedigree.

48:28.820 --> 48:29.180
Exactly.

48:29.720 --> 48:32.200
The framework is completely agnostic to the technology.

48:32.940 --> 48:38.640
If a traditional financial derivative traded by a white shoe investment bank in Manhattan produces no real world value,

48:38.920 --> 48:43.920
if it is just a closed loop casino that shifts systemic risks to the public taxpayers,

48:44.560 --> 48:48.640
it fails the exact same mathematical test as a dog themed crypto token.

48:49.360 --> 48:53.840
Pure redistribution is unproductive, whether it happens on a blockchain or on Wall Street.

48:54.200 --> 48:55.100
That is incredible.

48:55.100 --> 48:59.500
And the final objection is one we've largely covered, but it's worth reiterating.

49:00.140 --> 49:01.980
Proof of stake fixes all of this.

49:02.360 --> 49:07.020
And as we established, it fixes the energy externality, which lowers the resource costs.

49:07.160 --> 49:13.300
But it does absolutely nothing to fix the economic reality of the closed loop or the negative cash pool identity

49:13.300 --> 49:17.100
or the systemic shifting of failure costs onto local dependents.

49:17.100 --> 49:18.100
Wow.

49:18.100 --> 49:21.940
We have covered an immense amount of ground today.

49:21.940 --> 49:26.100
If you're listening, let's synthesize this journey.

49:26.820 --> 49:34.580
We started with a very simple intuitive assumption that a perfectly consistent mathematical log must be doing something inherently valuable.

49:34.580 --> 49:39.060
But we discovered that consistency does not equal contribution.

49:39.460 --> 49:43.420
A flawless ledger might just be recording the movement of empty boxes.

49:43.420 --> 49:49.800
We learned to measure true value, not by the noise a system makes or the billions of dollars of reported volume,

49:50.300 --> 49:51.700
but by what it displaces.

49:52.020 --> 49:53.420
The potato chip bag principle.

49:53.980 --> 49:56.620
We have to look at the accessible counterfactuals.

49:56.620 --> 50:04.860
We saw how closed loop systems where winners are paid entirely by the fresh deposits of losers drain real physical resources from the world.

50:05.100 --> 50:12.160
We looked at the arms race of mining and the 30,700 tons of e-waste dumped on the global domain.

50:13.020 --> 50:20.040
And we explored the devastating asymmetry of failure where diversified venture capitalists can profit off a portfolio of collapses,

50:20.040 --> 50:29.340
while local dependents bear the full unmitigated variance of a venture's death, highlighting the vital structural need for a mandatory closure reserve.

50:29.340 --> 50:32.820
So if you're listening, what does this all mean for you?

50:33.500 --> 50:45.120
It means that whenever you're presented with a shiny new technology, whether it is the next iteration of crypto or a massive new artificial intelligence paradigm or whatever Silicon Valley invents next,

50:45.120 --> 50:50.860
your first question should not be, wow, how does the underlying math work?

50:51.420 --> 51:00.360
Your first question must be, what independently existing real world human need does this fulfill cheaper and better than what we already have?

51:01.080 --> 51:09.960
You have to look past the technical admissibility of the system, look past the hype and demand rigorous mathematical proof of its external value.

51:10.460 --> 51:14.580
And I want to leave you with a final provocative thought to mull over on your own.

51:15.120 --> 51:23.680
We have talked a lot about decentralized tokens and blockchains today, but I want you to think about your own highly centralized digital life.

51:24.540 --> 51:27.060
Think about the social networks you log into every single day.

51:27.880 --> 51:33.560
Think about the cloud storage company that is currently holding 10 years of your family photos and personal documents.

51:33.980 --> 51:41.360
Those centralized companies also bundle your records, your payments, and your digital authority together, just like the tokens we analyzed.

51:41.360 --> 51:41.860
Exactly.

51:41.860 --> 51:48.440
If the company running your digital life decides that their servers are no longer profitable and they shut them down tomorrow,

51:49.240 --> 51:52.760
well, who holds the mandatory closure reserve for your memories?

51:53.520 --> 51:56.980
Do you have an unbundled independent record that survives their exit?

51:57.560 --> 52:05.180
Or are you just a local dependent tied to a chair at their roulette table waiting to see if they leave you with nothing but an empty vault?

52:05.180 --> 52:09.720
Keep questioning the real cost and the real structure of our digital world.

52:10.480 --> 52:11.960
Thanks for joining us on this deep dive.

